  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-344434",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-6x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x & =0 \\\\ 3x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,1],$ the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)^{3}-3(-1)^{2}+2=-1-3+2=-2 \\\\ f(0) & =(0)^{3}-3(0)^{2}+2=2 \\\\ f(1) & =(1)^{3}-3(1)^{2}+2=1-3+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(0)=2,$ so the global maximum is $2.$\n\n- The least value is $f(-1)=-2,$ so the global minimum is $-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14150956/assets/3939bd8d9b8efd0be9381087b9b1802ee9bebd4fad620b855904f87d3bc61a86.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
